        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Binary search: how many iterations in the worst case?",
          "content": "Each comparison **halves** the list. For 100 items: 100 → 50 → 25 → 13 → 7 → 4 → 2 → 1 → at most **7** comparisons, because 2⁷ = 128 ≥ 100. In general the worst case is ⌈log₂(n + 1)⌉ comparisons."
        },
        {
          "type": "comparison",
          "title": "Searching and sorting algorithms",
          "columns": [
            "Algorithm",
            "Best case",
            "Worst case",
            "Notes"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Linear search",
              "O(1) — first item",
              "O(n) — last item or absent",
              "Works on **unsorted** data"
            ],
            [
              "Binary search",
              "O(1) — middle item",
              "O(log n)",
              "Data must be **sorted**"
            ],
            [
              "Bubble sort",
              "O(n) — already sorted (with a \"swapped\" flag)",
              "O(n²)",
              "Simple; many swaps"
            ],
            [
              "Insertion sort",
              "O(n) — already sorted",
              "O(n²) — reverse order",
              "Good for small or nearly sorted lists"
